You are working on Little Pilot, a Godot 4 flying game for a 4-year-old, in this repo. Read README.md, docs/GAME_DESIGN.md, docs/DEVELOPMENT_ROADMAP.md and CREDITS.md before changing anything. I'm a beginner and mostly vibe coding, so explain what you did and why in plain language at the end of each phase.

## Ground rules (apply to every phase)
- Work one phase at a time, on its own git branch. Stop after each phase, summarise, and wait for me to say "continue".
- Run `./dev.sh check` and `./dev.sh test` before finishing each phase. All tests must pass.
- NEVER delete, skip, or loosen a test to make it pass. If a test fails, fix the code, or stop and explain to me why the test might be wrong.
- Don't rewrite systems that already work (flight controller, flight safety, save manager, state machine in main.gd) unless a phase requires it. Prefer small, additive changes.
- Performance: the target is the Steam Deck at 90 FPS with the Mobile renderer. Don't add effects that need Forward+-only features (e.g. the normal-roughness screen texture).
- Every new third-party asset must be added to CREDITS.md with author, source URL and licence before it's committed. Only use CC0 or CC-BY assets.
- Keep the game safe and gentle for a 4-year-old. No new failure states.

## Design decisions already made (don't change these)
- Crashing and the dragon blowing up the plane stay, and stay ON by default. My child asked for them and seeks them out. The parent settings already let them be switched off.
- No face or eyes on the plane. My child wants the plane to feel like a REAL plane. Prefer realism details over cartoon details for the aircraft.
- Voice narration is postponed. Don't add it.

## Phase 1: Safety net (CI)
1. Add a GitHub Actions workflow (.github/workflows/test.yml) that runs on every push and pull request:
   - Check out the repo with Git LFS, and cache the LFS objects (key the cache on the output of `git lfs ls-files -l`) so we don't burn GitHub's free LFS bandwidth.
   - Install the exact Godot version pinned in the README (standard, not .NET) in headless mode.
   - Import the project, then run the same tests `./dev.sh test` runs. dev.sh uses Flatpak Godot, so either make it respect the GODOT environment variable or call the tests directly. The job must fail if any test fails.
2. Add a second workflow that exports the Linux build when I push a git tag like v1.5.0, and attaches it to a GitHub Release.
3. Add a short "How CI works" section to the README in beginner-friendly language.

## Phase 2: Docs and housekeeping
1. Update README.md and docs/GAME_DESIGN.md so they match the real game: crashing and the dragon exist and are on by default, and parents can turn them off. Remove claims like "the plane can't crash" and "no failure states". Reword the philosophy as "failure is quick, silly and never punishing".
2. Delete the stale empty "(none yet)" asset table at the top of CREDITS.md.
3. Add an in-game Credits screen, reachable from the parent settings panel, that lists every CC-BY asset with its author and licence. It should be generated from data rather than hand-typed in a scene, so it's easy to keep in sync.

## Phase 3: Performance test
Extend the existing headless test setup with a frame-time benchmark: fly a fixed path through the fully grown city, the ocean, and Dragon Peak, and record the average and worst frame times. Print the results, and save them to a file so I can compare runs. Don't fail CI on it, since CI machines aren't a Steam Deck. Add `./dev.sh bench` to run it locally on the Deck.

## Phase 4: Visual consistency (not cartoon outlines)
The world mixes assets from Quaternius, Poly by Google, Sketchfab and KayKit, and their colours and shading clash. Goal: one cohesive, clean, slightly realistic look.
1. Move the Environment (sky, fog, glow, tonemapping, adjustments) from code in island_map.gd into a .tres resource the code loads, so I can tune it live in the editor. The game must look identical after this step.
2. Replace ProceduralSkyMaterial with a custom sky shader: a soft gradient, a sun disc with glow, and a subtle high-altitude cloud layer. Keep the current colours as the starting point.
3. At load or import time, give world assets a shared material treatment that harmonises them: consistent roughness, no shiny specular on cartoon assets, and gentle saturation and palette balancing. Do it automatically (an import script or a pass in asset_lib.gd), not by hand-editing each asset. No outlines on the aircraft.
4. Add a tonemapper toggle in the debug overlay (F3) that switches between Linear and AgX, so I can compare them on the Deck. Don't change the default.
5. Add vertex-shader wind sway to palms and pines.
6. After each step, run the benchmark and report the FPS impact.

## Phase 5: Make the plane feel real
1. The ailerons, elevator and rudder visibly move with stick input. Where a model's control surfaces aren't separate meshes, tell me which ones, and don't fake it badly.
2. Landing gear retracts after take-off and extends when flying low and slow near a landing area, if the models allow it. Otherwise tell me what model changes would be needed.
3. A cockpit camera view, added to the camera button cycle, with simple working gauges: speed, altitude, heading and a horizon.
4. Engine sound pitch and volume follow throttle and speed. Wind noise rises with speed.
5. Wingtip vapour trails in hard turns at speed, and a soft ground shadow or marker under the plane when it's low, to help with landing.

## Phase 6: Real sound effects
Replace the synthesised placeholder sounds in scripts/core/sfx.gd with real CC0 sounds (Kenney audio packs are a good source), stored in assets/audio/ under Git LFS and credited in CREDITS.md. Keep the synthesiser as a fallback if a file is missing. Keep the same Sfx.play() names so nothing else has to change. Make animals react to the honk: sheep scatter, cows moo, penguins slide, the whale spouts.

## Phase 7: New play
Do these as separate branches, in this order, and check with me before starting each one:
1. Sky-writing: hold a button to leave a coloured smoke trail (using the current paint colour) that lingers for about 60 seconds. Map it to a button that doesn't clash with the existing controls, and tell me which one you chose.
2. Sticker book: a picture-only book (opened from the pause menu) of discovered landmarks, animals and delivery recipients, saved through SaveManager with the same corrupt-file safety as everything else.
3. Parachute drops to boats and mermaids, reusing the delivery system.
4. A seaplane as a third aircraft that can land on and take off from the ocean, reusing the existing flight controller and the Gerstner waves.
5. Gentle weather: a rain cloud that washes the paint off when you fly through it, followed by a rainbow. No thunder.
